DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aaccll__eexxtteennddeedd__ffiillee__nnpp() function is a non-portable call that checks if
     the file or directory referred to by the argument _p_a_t_h___p contains ex‐
     tended access ACLs. The aaccll__eexxtteennddeedd__ffiillee__nnooffoollllooww__nnpp() function works
     the same way, except it does not follow symlinks. The
     aaccll__eexxtteennddeedd__lliinnkk__nnpp() function is a synonim to
     aaccll__eexxtteennddeedd__ffiillee__nnooffoollllooww__nnpp() named in FreeBSD style.  An ACL is con‐
     sidered to be extended access one if it contains entries other than the
     three required entries of tag types ACL_USER_OBJ, ACL_GROUP_OBJ and
     ACL_OTHER.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     Upon successful completion, this function returns 0 if the file object
     does not contain extended access ACLs and 1 in the other case.  Other‐
     wise, the value -1 is returned, and _e_r_r_n_o indicates the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     If any of the following conditions occur, the aaccll__eexxtteennddeedd__ffiillee__nnpp()
     function shall return a value of _-_1 and set _e_r_r_n_o to the corresponding
     value:

     [EACCES]           Search permission is denied for a component of the
                        path prefix.

